  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAnother clear and calm night is on tap tonight. This will bring\nanother chance for fog development across the region. With very\nefficient radiative cooling some areas may again see \ntemperatures drop into the upper 20s. In areas where these \noverlap, freezing fog development is favored. Some areas of \nwhich could become dense and lead to slick driving conditions, \nespecially on elevated surfaces such as bridges early Wednesday \nmorning. A joint probability using temperatures below freezing \nand a proxy for fog development heavily favors area river \nvalleys for freezing fog development. However, north of the \ncity, some low spots outside of our river valleys also sport \n~20-30% probabilities. Fog is expected to lift in the hours \nfollowing sunrise.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMid-level ridging will continue to shift eastward through the \nweek. This keeps the area dry and allows for a gradual warming \ntrend through the week. This warming trend will help push our \nhigh temperatures back well above normal for this time of year. \nBy Wednesday Pittsburgh sports a ~20% chance of high \ntemperatures over 60 degrees. By Thursday that jumps to >70% and\ncontinues to rise through Saturday. \n\nOur next chance for rain remains tied to the advance of a \ntrough from the central CONUS towards us this weekend. Clustered\nensembles continue to indicate a couple of waves moving across \nthe eastern CONUS. One tied to a coastal low looks to return \nrain along the Eastern Seaboard but likely doesn't affect us. \nModels differ on speed, depth and phasing of the second wave as \nit nears the Midwest. A deeper wave would be a slower but more\nlikely return of rain chances, while a quicker more subtle wave\nmay struggle to phase and could continue drier conditions. For \nnow we have maintained our low end chances of rain on Sunday.",
